= Information theory — Technical =

== Entropy ==
For discrete X:

<pre>
H(X) = -Σ_x p(x) log p(x)
</pre>

== Conditional entropy ==
<pre>
H(X|Y) = H(X,Y) - H(Y)
</pre>

== Mutual information ==
<pre>
I(X;Y) = H(X) + H(Y) - H(X,Y)
       = D_KL(P_XY || P_X P_Y)
</pre>

Mutual information is zero exactly when X and Y are independent under the usual discrete/continuous measure assumptions.

== Coding limits ==
Source coding relates expected code length to entropy.

Channel coding relates reliable transmission rate to channel capacity.

Rate–distortion theory studies lossy compression when controlled distortion is permitted.


== Divergence and channel capacity ==
For distributions p and q on the same discrete alphabet, <math>D_{KL}(p\|q)=\sum_xp(x)\log_2[p(x)/q(x)]</math> is nonnegative, but is generally asymmetric and not a metric. It is infinite when q assigns zero probability to an event of positive p probability. For a discrete memoryless channel with transition law W(y|x), capacity is <math>C=\max_{p(x)}I(X;Y)</math> bits per channel use. For a binary symmetric channel with independent crossover probability ε, uniform input achieves C=1−h₂(ε), where h₂ is binary entropy. The coding theorem concerns asymptotically reliable transmission at rates below capacity, not zero error at arbitrary finite block length.

== Coding and distortion ==
For an independently drawn discrete symbol and a binary prefix code, expected length L is bounded by H; a Shannon code achieves H≤L<H+1 for a nondegenerate finite source. Huffman coding optimizes expected length among binary prefix codes. Lossy coding replaces exact reconstruction with a distortion constraint; under a memoryless source model the rate–distortion function is the minimum mutual information over test channels satisfying the expected distortion bound. The distortion function is part of the problem specification, not a measure of semantic truth.

== Experimental estimation ==
Estimate empirical entropy from repeated samples with documented alphabet and sample size. Finite-sample plug-in estimates may be biased, especially for sparse distributions. Evaluate a communication code on independent messages under an explicit channel model and report error rate, throughput, latency and overhead separately. Statistical dependence does not imply causation.

